#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c20cd9a-5734-5129-93db-55f6dc985b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Formulas for Variation of Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "6060e2fe-05b3-587d-b5dd-3cdab705eb05" :question/math-academy-id "e-21546" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form $\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t)$, where $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for $\\mathbf x_p(t)$ is given below.\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{x}'_p(t) = \\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) + \\Phi' \\mathbf{u}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) + \\Phi' \\mathbf{u}(t) = A \\Phi \\mathbf{u}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi' = A \\Phi$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $\\Phi \\mathbf{u}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $\\mathbf{u}'(t) = \\Phi^{-1}(t) \\mathbf{f}(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L7}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\n> $\\textrm{L8}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\int \\Phi^{-1}\\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\n\nFill in the blanks with the correct reasons to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the fact that $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$ matrix.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L5}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$ both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\textrm{L7}$ into $AAAAAAAAA$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L4}$. Since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) =A\\mathbf{x}(t)$, i.e., by substituting any column $\\mathbf{c}(t)$ of $\\Phi$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n$\\mathbf{c}'(t) =A\\mathbf{c}(t)$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{c}'(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $\\Phi$. As a result, this means that $\\Phi' = A \\Phi$. Therefore, $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ$ matrix.\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L5}$ and $\\textrm{L6}$. Since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $\\Phi$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$ by $Φ^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n$Φ^{-1} \\cdot Φu^{′}(t)|= Φ^{-1} \\cdot f(t); (Φ^{-1}Φ) \\cdot u^{′}(t)|= Φ^{-1} \\cdot f(t); u^{′}(t)|= Φ^{-1} \\cdot f(t)$\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1}$, $\\textrm{L7}$, and $\\textrm{L8}$. Substituting\n$\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\nfrom $\\textrm{L7}$ into $\\textrm{L1}$, we get\n$x_{p}(t)|= Φu(t); = Φ∫Φ^{-1}f(t)dt$.\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\textrm{L7}$ into $L1$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "1d203940-c05d-5f30-a8ad-c618d6d6ba5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21547" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute $\\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t)$ and the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ of the system." :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2e^{-4t} \\\\ e^{3t} & -2e^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ^{-1} &= (1)/(0 \\cdot (-2e^{-4t}) - 2e^{-4t} \\cdot e^{3t})\\begin{bmatrix}-2e^{-4t} & -2e^{-4t} \\\\ -e^{3t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{e^{t}}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2e^{-4t} & -2e^{-4t} \\\\ -e^{3t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-3t} & e^{-3t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}e^{4t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nΦ^{-1}f(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-3t} & e^{-3t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}e^{4t} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 2e^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $\\mathbf{u}(t)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t) &= ∫Φ^{-1}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫\\begin{bmatrix}3e^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}dt \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}∫3e^{-2t}dt \\\\ ∫\\frac{1}{2}e^{5t}dt\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2}e^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{p}(t) &= Φu(t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2e^{-4t} \\\\ e^{3t} & -2e^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2}e^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}e^{t} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{10}e^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "ce663953-3e5e-55e2-a8db-6d7a531496cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-21548" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ |  | $\\boxed{\\rule{0pt}{1.3em}\\phantom{xxxxxxxx}}$ |  | . |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}_c(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}(t) = \\int \\Phi^{-1} \\, \\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ^{-1} &= \\frac{1}{e^{-2t} \\cdot e^{-4t} - 0}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\\nΦ^{-1}f(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2te^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $\\mathbf{u}(t)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t) &= ∫Φ^{-1}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫\\begin{bmatrix}2te^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}dt \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}∫2te^{2t}dt \\\\ ∫0dt\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=2t$ and $\\text{d}v=e^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$. Then, $\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2t}$. So,\n$∫2te^{2t}dt|= (2t) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}e^{2t} - ∫\\frac{1}{2}e^{2t}(2dt); = te^{2t} - ∫e^{2t}dt; = te^{2t} - \\frac{1}{2}e^{2t}; = \\frac{1}{2}e^{2t}(2t - 1)$.\n- For the second component, the integrand is zero, so the integral is zero.\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{p}(t) &= Φu(t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}e^{2t}(2t - 1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t - 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "60f42601-7e93-5cf0-afea-bec9263afe07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164041}] :question/id #uuid "a6dc5997-1d4e-5312-9245-798d648973c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-329817" :question/problem "$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t) + \\begin{bmatrix}12t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} +$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $-12t - 6$ |  |  |  |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c20cd9a-5734-5129-93db-55f6dc985b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Formulas for Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8608eb86-7846-5c0c-a0b9-5483f7d412bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164089} {:answer/id #uuid "e19ba397-1218-5049-897b-7ec948b40ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164090} {:answer/id #uuid "04bc74d5-6488-5f9c-89d3-179621597f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164091} {:answer/id #uuid "3a55a84a-9e03-57e1-aafb-531f05c56719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by $Φ^{− 1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164092}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d077fc5a-f837-562e-9934-ad718bd47619" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98df3017-3eb8-5507-8f5e-cc4b7f2bd8a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a fundamental" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164094} {:answer/id #uuid "00c41c96-848b-505c-873a-644562ac687f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is the identity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164095} {:answer/id #uuid "191f0574-5f11-578e-b6e8-ea18171c118d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is an invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164096} {:answer/id #uuid "5b15dec5-2127-5197-9d03-d4353079b93c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164097}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2080a27e-dd12-5e6a-b899-ff1fd5f8be7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164093} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2041cd9-ed52-5f7d-84eb-36b642efe80e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164099} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f76d10-414d-5b54-82d2-c7d0803a3f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164100} {:answer/id #uuid "0accd6ae-e802-5ddb-82ef-e55bdbe02f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164101} {:answer/id #uuid "5976edc2-fa65-5378-8a1b-b7af3df0e7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by $Φ^{− 1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164102}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8261617f-ecee-5fd9-a564-c41fdc6ecae6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164098}] :question/id #uuid "9ad3fcdc-246d-580f-b053-3062ecfa179a" :question/math-academy-id "q-330877" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n$x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + f(t)$\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form $x_{p}(t) = Φu(t)$, where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for $x_{p}(t)$ is given below.\n\n> $L1$: $x_{p}(t) = Φu(t)$\n> $L2$: $x_{p}^{′}(t) = Φu^{′}(t) + Φ^{′}u(t)$\n> $L3$: $Φu^{′}(t) + Φ^{′}u(t) = AΦu(t) + f(t)$\n> $L4$: $Φ^{′} = AΦ$\n> $L5$: $Φu^{′}(t) = f(t)$\n> $L6$: $u^{′}(t) = Φ^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n> $L7$: $u(t) = ∫Φ^{-1}f(t)dt$\n> $L8$: $x_{p}(t) = Φ∫Φ^{-1}f(t)dt$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\nLine $L6$ follows from line $L5$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\nLine $L7$ follows from line $L6$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomoge